    "evidence_text": "FLC is a fragment-level task that asks for the identification of propagandist text fragments in a news article and also for the prediction of the specific propaganda technique used in each such fragment (18-way classification task).\n\nSLC is a sentence-level binary classification task asking to detect the sentences that contain propaganda.\n\nIntroduction\tPropaganda aims at influencing people's mindset with the purpose of advancing a specific agenda.\n\nIn the Internet era, thanks to the mechanism of sharing in social networks, propaganda campaigns have the potential of reaching very large audiences , , .\n\nPropagandist news articles use specific techniques to convey their message, such as whataboutism, red Herring, and name calling, among many others (cf. Section SECREF3).\n\nWhereas proving intent is not easy, we can analyse the language of a claim/article and look for the use of specific propaganda techniques.\n\nThe task is based on a corpus of news articles annotated with an inventory of 18 propagandist techniques at the fragment level.\n\nRelated Work\tPropaganda has been tackled mostly at the article level.\n\nthat corpus: propaganda vs. the other three categories.\n\nannotated a large binary corpus of propagandist vs. non-propagandist articles and proposed a feature-based system for discriminating between them.\n\n,  introduced Argotario, a game to educate people to recognize and create fallacies, a by-product of which is a corpus with $1.3k$ arguments annotated with five fallacies such as ad hominem, red herring and irrelevant authority, which directly relate to propaganda.\n\nUnlike , , , our corpus uses 18 techniques annotated on the same set of news articles.\n\nPropaganda Techniques\tPropaganda uses psychological and rhetorical techniques to achieve its objective.\n\nSuch techniques include the use of logical fallacies and appeal to emotions.\n\nWe refer the reader to  for more details on the propaganda techniques; below we report the list of techniques:\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 1.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 2.\n\nLabeling the object of the propaganda as something the target audience fears, hates, finds undesirable or otherwise loves or praises .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 3.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 4.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 5.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 6.\n\nSeeking to build support for an idea by instilling anxiety and/or panic in the population towards an alternative, possibly based on preconceived judgments.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 7.\n\nPlaying on strong national feeling (or with respect to a group, e.g., race, gender, political preference) to justify or promote an action or idea .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 8.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 9.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 10.\n\nauthority/expert, although it is referred to as testimonial in the literature .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 11. Black-and-white fallacy, dictatorship.\tPresenting two alternative options as the only possibilities, when in fact more possibilities exist . As an extreme case, telling the audience exactly what actions to take, eliminating any other possible choice (dictatorship).\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 12. Thought-terminating clich\u00e9.\tWords or phrases that discourage critical thought and meaningful discussion about a given topic. They are typically short and generic sentences that offer seemingly simple answers to complex questions or that distract attention away from other lines of thought .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 13. Whataboutism.\tDiscredit an opponent's position by charging them with hypocrisy without directly disproving their argument .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 14. Reductio ad Hitlerum.\tPersuading an audience to disapprove an action or idea by suggesting that the idea is popular with groups hated in contempt by the target audience. It can refer to any person or concept with a negative connotation .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 15. Red herring.\tIntroducing irrelevant material to the issue being discussed, so that everyone's attention is diverted\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 16.\n\nAttempting to persuade the target audience to join in and take the course of action because \u201ceveryone else is taking the same action\u201d .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 17.\n\nUsing deliberately unclear words, to let the audience have its own interpretation , .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 18.\n\nWhen an opponent's proposition is substituted with a similar one which is then refuted in place of the original .\n\nGiven a news article, detect all spans of the text in which a propaganda technique is used.\n\nIn addition, for each span the propaganda technique applied must be identified.\n\nA sentence is considered propagandist if it contains at least one propagandist fragment.\n\nFigure FIGREF15 shows an annotated example, which contains several propaganda techniques.\n\nFLC is a composition of two subtasks: the identification of the propagandist text fragments and the identification of the techniques used (18-way classification task).",
